\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{The shorted operator along a subspace}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{Let #{V} be finite-dimensional over an ordered field #{F},
with a specified positive symmetric pairing #{h}. For a
\vocabk{nonnegative self-adjoint operator}{fdip-00K1} #{A:V\to V}
and subspace #{X\subseteq V}, put
##{\begin{aligned}
\mathcal C_X(A)=\{T:V\to V:\ &T\text{ is self-adjoint},\\
 &0\leq T\leq A,\quad\im T\subseteq X\}.
\end{aligned}}
A \newvocab{shorted operator} of #{A} along #{X} is a greatest
member #{T_0} of this set in the Loewner order: #{T_0\in\mathcal C_X(A)}
and #{T\leq T_0} for every #{T\in\mathcal C_X(A)}.
The term specifies a property if such a member exists;
\ref{fdip-00K8} proves existence and uniqueness in this setting.}

\p{This order-theoretic definition uses the comparison just
established in \ref{fdip-00K3}. Its finite ordered-field realization
is obtained by the minimizing-extension calculation of
\ref{fdip-00K7}.}
